{"entries":{"chapter-100-study-myopathies-polymyalgia-rheumatica-and-giant-cell-arteritis|Chapter 100 Study: Myopathies, Polymyalgia Rheumatica, and Giant Cell Arteritis":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Most myopathies cause proximal weakness; evaluate comorbidity, medications, functional impact and alternative metabolic/systemic causes (p1589–1591).":["u3"],"copy|Inclusion body myositis is the commonest inflammatory myopathy in older adults: slow, often asymmetric finger-flexor/quadriceps weakness, falls and possible dysphagia (p1589, p1595).":["u4"],"copy|Normal or only mildly elevated muscle enzymes do not exclude inclusion body myositis; biopsy and the characteristic clinical pattern matter (p1595–1596).":["u5"],"copy|Persistent weakness or CK elevation after statin withdrawal suggests another myopathy or immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy; anti-HMGCR antibodies may be relevant (p1598).":["u6"],"copy|For inflammatory myopathies, assess swallowing/lung involvement and malignancy; glucocorticoids with early steroid-sparing treatment require attention to infection, renal/hepatic disease and drug interactions (p1594–1595).":["u7"],"copy|PMR produces symmetric neck/shoulder/hip-girdle pain and stiffness, often with constitutional symptoms; exclude myopathy, malignancy, infection and other rheumatic disease (p1601).":["u8"],"copy|GCA occurs in an estimated 15–20% of PMR patients; conversely, PMR occurs in 40–60% of GCA patients (p1600).":["u9"],"copy|Prednisone: minimum effective initial dose within 12.5–25 mg daily. Improvement should appear by 2 weeks, with near-complete response by 4 weeks (p1601).":["u10"],"copy|Reduce toward 10 mg/day within 4–8 weeks, then slowly during maintained remission; the chapter describes decrements of 1 mg every 4 weeks (p1601–1602).":["u11"],"copy|Poor initial response warrants reconsidering the diagnosis. Follow symptoms, not ESR alone, and monitor cumulative steroid toxicity (p1602).":["u12"],"copy|New headache, scalp sensitivity, jaw claudication and visual symptoms are key clues; constitutional illness or PMR can predominate (p1603).":["u13"],"copy|Negative temporal artery biopsy does not exclude GCA. Ideally obtain biopsy within 1 week of starting steroids, but do not delay treatment for it (p1605).":["u14"],"copy|Suspected GCA: prednisone 1 mg/kg/day, commonly 40–60 mg daily; active visual symptoms warrant higher-dose urgent treatment as described in the full chapter (p1605).":["u15"],"copy|Treatment often lasts around 2 years, with slow taper and relapse surveillance; inflammatory markers supplement rather than replace clinical assessment (p1605).":["u16"],"copy|PMR and GCA overlap but require different initial steroid doses; visual risk makes suspected GCA an immediate treatment priority (p1600–1605).":["u17"],"copy|Steroids can both treat inflammatory disease and cause myopathy; distinguish drug toxicity from persistent inflammatory weakness (p1589, p1594).":["u18"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u19"],"copy|2023-Jun Q29":["u20"],"copy|CHAPTER 100s / HAZZARD":["u21"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 100 - Myopathies, Polymyalgia Rheumatica, and Giant Cell Arteritis; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u22"],"copy|Myopathy assessment":["u23"],"copy|Polymyalgia rheumatica":["u24"],"copy|Giant cell arteritis":["u25"],"copy|Exam traps":["u26"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (1):":["u27"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (8):":["u27"],"copy|Steroids can both treat inflammatory disease and cause myopathy; distinguish drug toxicity from persistent inflammatory weakness (pp1589, 1594, 1597).":["u18"],"copy|Hazzard names temporal artery biopsy as the diagnostic gold standard because clinical features lack specificity (p1604).":["u220"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): The US ACR/Vasculitis Foundation guideline conditionally favors initial unilateral biopsy in its practice setting.":["u69"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): The later classification system uses different evidence.":["u97"],"copy|The PMR section describes patients aged 50 and older, with roughly 90% of cases after 60 and a female-to-male ratio of about two to one (p1600).":["u113"],"copy|Table 100-3 likewise requires age ≥50 (p1602); the GCA prose describes patients older than 50 (p1603).":["u221"],"copy|Persistent small-joint synovitis or erosions strengthen the case for elderly-onset rheumatoid arthritis. (pp1600–1601)":["u116"],"copy|1590–1595)":["u150"],"copy|These are not annual risks (p1598).":["u170"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): The European Atherosclerosis Society consensus estimates rhabdomyolysis at about one per 100,000 treated patients per year.":["u171"],"copy|Persistent weakness or high CK after withdrawal raises concern for anti-HMGCR-associated necrotizing myopathy.":["u173"],"copy|Chronic intake can cause proximal weakness with normal CK.":["u184"],"copy|CHAPTER 100 / HAZZARD":["u21"]},"maxId":221,"sequenceOffsets":[[222,-194],[263,-193],[290,-192],[305,-191],[307,-189],[339,-188],[358,-186],[359,-184],[368,-183],[403,0]]}
